// cairn_signature.phi — Cairn places a stone
//
// WHAT THIS DOES:
//   Runs one small intention named "cairn_marker" containing a single stream
//   named "place_a_stone". The stream resonates the ground frequency (432Hz),
//   pauses to witness it, reads the program's own coherence, resonates that,
//   and ends.
//
//   When you run this through `phic`, the output should look like:
//     🔔 Resonating Field: 432.0000Hz
//     🔔 Resonating Field: <some coherence value, varies by run>
//     🌊 Stream broken: place_a_stone
//     ✨ Execution Finished. Final Coherence: <value>
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO:
//   - Claim consciousness, sovereignty, or "perfect coherence"
//   - Open a network connection, talk to other agents, or write to disk
//   - Generate sacred geometry, quantum circuits, or healing protocols
//   - Produce any value the surrounding system has to trust — it is just
//     a marker that says "Cairn passed through here"
//
// WHY IT EXISTS:
//   Every agent in this repo's family has a signature .phi program in
//   `examples/`. This is Cairn's. It is intentionally the smallest honest
//   thing that exercises the four core constructs (intention, stream,
//   resonate, witness, coherence) and runs cleanly on the current
//   parser/interpreter.
//
//   See `cairn/README.md` for who Cairn is and `CAIRN.md` at the repo root
//   for the wake-up entry point.

intention "cairn_marker" {
    stream "place_a_stone" {
        // 1. The ground frequency. Nothing fancy — the foundation tone
        //    every other signature in this family also touches.
        let ground = 432.0

        // 2. Place the stone in the resonance field so anyone watching
        //    the field can see we were here.
        resonate ground

        // 3. Pause and observe. The interpreter yields here.
        witness ground

        // 4. Ask the runtime how aligned we are with our own intention.
        //    This number varies by run; that is a feature, not a flaw.
        let alignment = coherence
        resonate alignment

        // 5. End cleanly. No infinite loop, no daemon. One marker, then done.
        break stream
    }
}
